 ## Overview
-Ray Direction Jitter is a technique that improves the generalizability of learned rendering models by applying sub-pixel noise to ray directions [S1, S2]. It prevents the color network from overfitting to specific training viewing directions, which otherwise leads to artifacts in novel view synthesis and texture reproduction [S2]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md) which distributes samples across a pixel area, this method perturbs the ray vector itself while ensuring the ray remains confined within the pixel's geometric boundaries [S1].
+Ray Direction Jitter is a technique that improves the generalizability of learned rendering models by applying sub-pixel noise to ray directions [S1, S2]. It prevents the color network from overfitting to specific training viewing directions, which otherwise leads to artifacts in novel view synthesis and texture reproduction [S2]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md) which distributes samples across a pixel area, this method perturbs the ray vector itself while ensuring the ray remains confined within the pixel's geometric boundaries [S1, G].
 
 ## How it works
